-- 24 Nov 2026: deepen six practical learning guides that Search Console shows impressions for.
-- The five articles added on 23 Nov were 280-390 words and the Arabic guide about 600; each now
-- has worked examples, a realistic routine and a self-check, still under the owner's 1,000-word
-- editorial maximum. Practical guidance only: no religious rulings, quotations, prices, credentials
-- or outcome promises. One trial link per article; links go to routes that exist.
-- Idempotent: a row is rewritten only while its body differs from the text below.

with v(slug, reading_minutes, body_html) as (values

('can-i-learn-quran-by-myself', 4, $b$<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> you can practise a lot by yourself, such as revising letters, listening to recitation and reviewing what you have learned. Reading accurately and pronouncing letters correctly are hard to check alone, so most learners do best by adding regular feedback from a teacher.</p>
<p>People who ask "can I learn the Quran by myself?" are usually busy, shy about starting, or unsure where to begin. Self-study is a good habit; the question is which parts it covers well and which parts need another set of ears.</p>
<h2>What works well alone</h2><ul><li>Reviewing Arabic letters and short vowels with a workbook or chart.</li><li>Listening to a recitation while following the text with your finger.</li><li>Repeating a short section you have already been taught.</li><li>Reading a translation you understand to follow the meaning of a passage.</li><li>Keeping a simple log of what you practised and what felt difficult.</li></ul>
<h2>What is hard to judge alone</h2><p>You cannot easily hear your own mistakes. Letters such as ث, س and ص, or ح, ه and خ, can sound alike to a new learner, and a small error can be repeated for weeks because nothing points it out. Recordings and apps can show you a model, but a recording does not tell you which sound you actually made. That is where a teacher's correction saves time and stops habits from setting in.</p>
<h2>Three common self-study traps</h2><ol><li><strong>Relying on transliteration.</strong> Arabic sounds written in English letters are only approximate, so you may learn a pronunciation that is hard to change later.</li><li><strong>Skipping the basics.</strong> Moving to long passages before letters and vowel marks feel steady can lead to guessing rather than reading.</li><li><strong>Listening without reading.</strong> Following the text while you listen connects what you hear to what you see.</li></ol>
<h2>A way to check yourself between lessons</h2><p>Record yourself reading one short line, then play it back beside a recitation you trust. Note two or three differences: a letter that sounded different, or a vowel held too long or too short. You will not always be able to name the fix, but a note like this gives a teacher something specific to correct.</p>
<h2>A realistic week that mixes both</h2><ul><li>One lesson each week for new material and correction.</li><li>Ten to fifteen minutes on most days, repeating only what the teacher assigned.</li><li>Once a week, record one line and write down one question.</li></ul>
<p>Fifteen focused minutes usually does more than one long session now and then. The <a href="/blog/printable-weekly-quran-practice-planner">printable weekly Quran practice planner</a> can help you keep track.</p>
<h2>How do you know if you need a teacher?</h2><p>Ask yourself three questions. Could someone who reads well confirm that you pronounce each letter correctly? Do you know why a sound is stretched or shortened where it is? Can you tell when you have read a word incorrectly? If the answer to any of these is "I am not sure", a short lesson can show you where you stand.</p>
<h2>Start at the right level</h2><p>If you are not sure what you can read, <a href="/blog/noorani-qaida-or-quran-reading">this guide to choosing between Noorani Qaida and Quran reading</a> explains what to look for. Adults starting from the beginning can follow <a href="/blog/adult-quran-beginner-first-month">a realistic first month</a>, and those who want to understand the Arabic can read the <a href="/blog/quranic-arabic-roadmap-first-year">Quranic Arabic roadmap</a>.</p>
<h2>Ask for feedback early</h2><p>Even if you plan to study mostly alone, a short trial lesson can show whether your reading is on track and what to practise next. You can <a href="/free-trial">request a free trial on an eligible plan</a> and bring a few lines you have been practising.</p>$b$),

('noorani-qaida-for-adults-online', 4, $b$<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Noorani Qaida is a reading foundation, and adults of any background can start it. It teaches letters, vowel marks and how sounds join, usually through short exercises with a teacher who corrects your pronunciation as you go.</p>
<p>Many adults search for "Noorani Qaida for adults online" because they feel they should already be able to read. Beginning as an adult is common, and starting with the basics is a practical choice rather than a step back.</p>
<h2>What Noorani Qaida usually covers</h2><p>Plans differ, but a Qaida course commonly moves through the letters and their sounds, short vowel marks, joined letters, long vowels, the sign for no vowel, doubled letters and the endings that add an "n" sound. Each step builds on the one before, which is why a teacher usually checks one stage before opening the next.</p>
<h2>What a first lesson can look like</h2><p>The teacher listens to what you can already recognise, then picks a starting exercise. You may repeat letters, practise short vowels and notice differences between similar sounds. Expect corrections to be short and specific, such as "this letter comes from further back in the throat".</p>
<h2>Sounds adults often find tricky</h2><p>Several Arabic sounds have no close match in English, so they take repeated listening. Common pairs to practise slowly are ث and س, ح and ه, ق and ك, and ع and ء. Your teacher can show where each sound is made and how to tell the pair apart when you hear them.</p>
<h2>A possible first month</h2><p>Every learner moves at a different pace, so treat this as a sketch, not a schedule.</p><ul><li><strong>Week one:</strong> recognise letters and say each sound clearly.</li><li><strong>Week two:</strong> read letters with short vowels.</li><li><strong>Week three:</strong> join letters into short words.</li><li><strong>Week four:</strong> meet long vowels and review everything aloud.</li></ul>
<h2>Fit practice into a working week</h2><ul><li>Choose a fixed ten to fifteen minutes you can repeat.</li><li>Practise the same few lines until they feel steady before moving on.</li><li>Note which sounds you mix up and ask about them next lesson.</li></ul>
<h2>Take away the pressure</h2><p>You do not need to compare yourself with children or with other adults. Progress depends on your starting point and how often you practise. Ask the teacher how they will show you that you are ready for the next step; see <a href="/blog/how-long-to-learn-noorani-qaida">how long Noorani Qaida can take</a> for what affects the pace, and the <a href="/learn/quran-for-adults">guides for adult learners</a> for more ideas.</p>
<h2>After Qaida</h2><p>Once letters and sounds feel steady, many learners move to supported Quran reading with Tajweed feedback. Compare <a href="/courses/noorani-qaida">Noorani Qaida</a> with <a href="/courses/quran-nazara">Quran Nazara</a>, and read <a href="/blog/noorani-qaida-or-quran-reading">where to start</a> if you are unsure.</p>
<h2>Common questions from adult beginners</h2><p><strong>Do I need to know any Arabic first?</strong> No. Noorani Qaida assumes you are starting from the letters.</p><p><strong>Will I be taught with children's material?</strong> Ask the teacher how lessons are adapted. Adults often move faster through some exercises and need longer on pronunciation, so the pace should follow you.</p><p><strong>How often should I take lessons?</strong> Two or three lessons a week with short daily practice suits many adults; a lighter schedule works if it is one you can keep. Ask the coordinator what is available for your time zone.</p><p><strong>What if I forget between lessons?</strong> That is normal. The teacher can repeat the last exercise at the start of the next lesson before adding anything new.</p>
<h2>Begin with a trial</h2><p>You can <a href="/free-trial">request a free trial on an eligible plan</a> and tell the teacher you are an adult beginner so the first lesson matches your pace.</p>$b$),

('quran-study-for-new-muslims', 4, $b$<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> new Muslims can begin with reading the Arabic letters at a gentle pace, alongside a translation in a language they know. A patient teacher, short regular practice and permission to take your time matter more than speed.</p>
<p>People who search for "Quran for reverts" are often balancing a new faith, work and family. There is no race. This guide offers practical steps; it does not replace advice from your local community or a knowledgeable teacher on questions of faith.</p>
<h2>Start with the letters</h2><p>Reading Arabic can feel unfamiliar at first. <a href="/courses/noorani-qaida">Noorani Qaida</a> introduces letters and sounds in small steps. Tell the teacher you are new so the pace and explanations suit you.</p>
<h2>Keep meaning close</h2><p>Reading aloud and understanding are different skills. A translation can help you follow what you read; <a href="/courses/quran-translation">Quran Translation lessons</a> and our guide to <a href="/blog/quran-translation-tafseer-beginners">translation and Tafseer for beginners</a> explain how they work. Choose one translation to read consistently and ask your teacher or community which one they suggest, so you are not comparing several at once.</p>
<h2>A gentle first month</h2><p>Treat this as a sketch to adjust, not a target.</p><ul><li><strong>Week one:</strong> ten minutes a day on letters and their sounds, with a translation read once a week.</li><li><strong>Week two:</strong> add short vowel marks and read a few letters together.</li><li><strong>Week three:</strong> repeat what you have been taught before adding anything new.</li><li><strong>Week four:</strong> review aloud with your teacher and decide the next small step together.</li></ul>
<h2>If you feel behind</h2><p>Many new Muslims feel they should already know what others seem to know. Learners grew up in different settings, and where you start does not say how far you will go. Short sessions you can repeat usually feel better than long sessions you dread. The <a href="/learn/quran-for-reverts">guide for new Muslims</a> collects more first steps.</p>
<h2>Pace yourself</h2><ul><li>Choose a short daily time, even ten minutes.</li><li>Repeat what you have been taught before adding new material.</li><li>Write down questions to ask your teacher instead of searching late at night.</li></ul>
<h2>Ask questions in the right place</h2><p>For religious questions beyond reading practice, ask how the academy refers them to a suitably knowledgeable teacher, and speak to your local community too. A reading teacher's role is to help you recite and understand the text, not to give personal rulings.</p>
<h2>Choose a teacher you feel comfortable with</h2><p>Ask about teacher preference, lesson time and how corrections are given. <a href="/blog/questions-before-quran-course-enrolment">Questions to ask before enrolling in a Quran course</a> is a useful checklist. If you are returning after a gap, <a href="/blog/returning-to-quran-study-adults">returning to Quran study as an adult</a> may also help.</p>
<h2>Common questions</h2><p><strong>Should I learn to read before I learn the meaning?</strong> Many learners do both at once: a little reading practice each day and a translation passage each week, so neither feels like a barrier.</p><p><strong>Is it a problem if my pronunciation is not perfect?</strong> Everyone starts somewhere. A teacher corrects one or two things at a time, and steady practice improves reading gradually.</p><p><strong>Can I learn with a teacher who speaks my language?</strong> Ask during the trial. Explanations in a language you know can make early lessons much easier.</p><p><strong>What if I have no one at home to practise with?</strong> Record a short line and bring it to the next lesson. Your teacher can listen and give feedback.</p>
<h2>Try a first lesson</h2><p>You can <a href="/free-trial">request a free trial on an eligible plan</a> and mention that you are new to the Quran. There is no pressure to arrive knowing anything.</p>$b$),

('quranic-arabic-roadmap-first-year', 5, $b$<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> learn Quranic Arabic in this order: read the script accurately, build a bank of common Quranic words, understand short phrases in passages you already recite, and only then add grammar in small steps. Keep reviewing a few words often rather than studying many at once.</p>
<p>"Learning Quranic Arabic" is one of the most searched phrases around this topic, yet learners often start with grammar books and stall. A clear order keeps motivation up. This roadmap is a planning aid, not a promise of a level by a date.</p>
<h2>Step 1: Read the script with confidence</h2><p>Before vocabulary, make sure you can recognise letters, vowel marks and joined forms. If this is still uncertain, begin with <a href="/courses/noorani-qaida">Noorani Qaida</a> or supported reading in <a href="/courses/quran-nazara">Quran Nazara</a>.</p>
<h2>Step 2: Collect common Quranic words</h2><p>Start with short, frequent words from passages you already know by heart or recite often. Write each word with its meaning and the place you met it. Take the opening words of Surah al-Fatihah as a model: <em>al-hamd</em> (praise), <em>rabb</em> (Lord) and <em>al-ʿalamin</em> (the worlds). Review the list for a few minutes most days.</p>
<h2>Step 3: Notice word families</h2><p>Many Arabic words are built from a three-letter root, and words that share a root share a core idea. The root ك ت ب (k-t-b) gives <em>kitab</em> (book), <em>katib</em> (writer) and <em>maktab</em> (desk or office). The root ر ح م (r-h-m) gives <em>rahma</em> (mercy), and the two names heard in the opening phrase of most surahs, <em>ar-Rahman</em> and <em>ar-Rahim</em>, come from it. Seeing a root you have met before makes a new word less strange. Ask your teacher to point out roots in your passages.</p>
<h2>Step 4: Read short phrases with meaning</h2><p>Take a short passage, read it aloud, then match words to the meanings in your list. Compare with a translation to check your understanding. If you want meaning soon, <a href="/courses/quran-translation">Quran Translation</a> runs alongside Arabic study; see <a href="/blog/quran-translation-and-arabic-difference">how translation and Arabic learning goals differ</a>.</p>
<h2>Step 5: Add grammar gradually</h2><p>Introduce word patterns and sentence structure one idea at a time, using examples from passages you know. A teacher can explain why a form changes, which is hard to work out alone. The <a href="/courses/arabic-language">Arabic Language course</a> covers this kind of structured study.</p>
<h2>A year in four stages</h2><ul><li><strong>First quarter:</strong> steady reading and a small word list.</li><li><strong>Second quarter:</strong> a growing list and word families from familiar passages.</li><li><strong>Third quarter:</strong> short phrases and first grammar ideas, with a teacher.</li><li><strong>Fourth quarter:</strong> longer passages with support and a review of what you can follow.</li></ul>
<p>Speed varies with experience and time, so adjust the stages rather than measuring yourself against them.</p>
<h2>Weekly habits that help</h2><ul><li>Five to ten minutes of word review most days.</li><li>One short passage read slowly each week.</li><li>One question list for your teacher.</li></ul>
<h2>Mistakes that slow learners down</h2><ul><li><strong>Memorising long word lists with no context.</strong> Words stick better when tied to a passage you know.</li><li><strong>Starting with advanced grammar books.</strong> They assume vocabulary you have not built yet.</li><li><strong>Studying in long, rare bursts.</strong> A few minutes most days beats a weekend of cramming.</li><li><strong>Mixing too many resources.</strong> Choose one set of materials with your teacher and finish it.</li><li><strong>Never speaking or reading aloud.</strong> Say each new word and phrase out loud so it becomes familiar.</li></ul>
<h2>Where to go next</h2><p>Our guide on <a href="/blog/arabic-for-quran-readers">learning Arabic as a Quran reader</a> explains how language study differs from reading practice. When you are ready to discuss your level, <a href="/free-trial">request a free trial on an eligible plan</a> and describe what you can already read.</p>$b$),

('online-quran-classes-for-women-and-girls', 4, $b$<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> women and girls can learn the Quran online one-to-one from home. Before enrolling, ask whether you can request a female teacher, which lesson times fit your day, how the lesson is set up for privacy, and how your progress is reported.</p>
<p>Many learners search for "ladies Quran classes near me" because they want a comfortable setting and a teacher they feel at ease with. Online lessons can offer that without travel, but the details differ between academies, so it helps to ask clearly.</p>
<h2>Ask about teacher preference</h2><p>If you or your daughter would be more comfortable with a female teacher, say so at the start and ask whether this can be arranged for your level and lesson times. On the free trial form, open "Add class preferences" and choose a teacher preference; the coordinator then confirms availability. Availability depends on the course and the schedule, so ask for an honest answer rather than assuming. Our page on <a href="/learn/female-quran-teacher">learning with a female Quran teacher</a> explains more.</p>
<h2>Choose lesson times you can keep</h2><p>A routine you can keep matters more than a perfect slot. Mothers often plan around school runs and meals; students plan around classes and homework. Share your time zone and the hours that are realistically free, then ask which times a teacher could offer each week. Our guide to <a href="/blog/returning-to-quran-study-adults">returning to Quran study after a break</a> covers how to restart gently.</p>
<h2>Set up the lesson space</h2><p>Choose a quiet corner, check your microphone and headphones, and decide whether the camera will be on. <a href="/blog/online-quran-lesson-setup-accessibility">Setting up an online lesson that is easier to follow</a> lists simple checks.</p>
<h2>Questions for parents of girls</h2><ul><li>Can a parent sit in or stay nearby during the lesson?</li><li>Who receives the lesson link, and who else can join?</li><li>How are concerns raised, and who answers them?</li><li>Are lessons recorded, and if so, who can see the recording?</li></ul>
<h2>Match the course to your starting point</h2><ul><li>New to Arabic letters: ask about <a href="/courses/noorani-qaida">Noorani Qaida</a>.</li><li>Can read but want correct pronunciation: ask about <a href="/courses/tajweed">Tajweed</a> and <a href="/courses/quran-nazara">Quran Nazara</a>.</li><li>Want to understand meaning: ask about <a href="/courses/quran-translation">Quran Translation</a>.</li></ul>
<h2>Ask how progress is shared</h2><p>Ask how often you will hear about progress, what the teacher will practise with you between lessons, and how to raise a concern. A short written note after a few lessons helps parents and adult learners see what is improving.</p>
<h2>What to prepare for the first lesson</h2><ul><li>Your time zone and the days you are free.</li><li>What the learner can read now, even if only a few letters.</li><li>One goal, such as reading with confidence or understanding meaning.</li><li>Any questions about the teacher, privacy or the lesson link.</li></ul>
<h2>Common questions</h2><p><strong>Can classes be one-to-one?</strong> Ask for a one-to-one lesson if you want one; the coordinator can tell you what the plan offers.</p><p><strong>Can I join with my daughter?</strong> Ask whether a parent can sit in, and whether you can have your own lesson at a different time.</p><p><strong>What if my schedule changes?</strong> Ask how rescheduling works and how much notice is expected, so you know before you start.</p><p><strong>Do classes cover Tajweed?</strong> Many courses include correction of pronunciation. Say what you want to improve and ask how it will be taught.</p>
<h2>Try before you commit</h2><p>A trial lesson lets you meet the teacher and check the sound, pace and comfort level. You can <a href="/free-trial">request a free trial on an eligible plan</a> and mention your teacher preference when you do.</p>$b$),

('arabic-for-quran-readers', 4, $b$<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> to learn Arabic for understanding the Quran, first make sure you can read the Arabic script accurately, then study Quranic vocabulary and grammar with a teacher, starting from short passages you already recite. Review a few words often, and keep translation study alongside so you follow the meaning while your Arabic grows.</p>
<p>Many learners who can read the Quran aloud later want to understand the Arabic itself. This guide explains how Arabic language study differs from reading practice and from translation, and which questions help you choose a sensible starting point.</p>
<p>Choosing a teacher? See <a href="/blog/native-arabic-speaking-quran-teacher">whether you need a native Arabic-speaking Quran teacher</a>.</p>
<h2>Reading the script is not the same as understanding the language</h2><p>A learner may read fluently and accurately yet not recognise the meaning of the words. Reading lessons such as <a href="/courses/quran-nazara">Quran Nazara</a> focus on correct reading. The <a href="/courses/arabic-language">Arabic Language course</a> is a separate discussion about vocabulary, word forms and sentence structure. Both are valuable; they build different skills.</p>
<h2>Arabic study and translation answer different needs</h2><p>Translation lessons discuss meaning in a language the learner already knows. Arabic study aims to help the learner recognise words and structures in the original text over time. If your main goal is to follow the meaning of passages soon, discuss <a href="/courses/quran-translation">Quran Translation</a>. If you want to build language knowledge gradually, discuss Arabic. The comparison of <a href="/blog/quran-translation-and-arabic-difference">translation and Arabic learning goals</a> covers this choice in more detail.</p>
<h2>Ask which kind of Arabic the course teaches</h2><p>Arabic is taught in different forms for different purposes. Ask the teacher whether lessons focus on the Arabic of the Quran and classical texts, on modern written Arabic, or on a spoken variety, and why that suits your goal. A learner who wants to understand Quranic vocabulary and grammar needs a plan built around that aim.</p>
<h2>Questions to ask before starting</h2><ul><li>How will my current reading level be assessed?</li><li>Which materials are used, and are they linked to Quranic passages?</li><li>How is vocabulary practised between lessons?</li><li>When will grammar be introduced, and how is it explained?</li><li>How will progress be reviewed?</li></ul>
<h2>Build vocabulary from passages you already know</h2><p>Many learners find it easier to start with short passages they have already heard or recited. Pick a few words and ask the teacher to show where else they appear, what pattern they follow and how they change within a sentence. Seeing the same word in several places helps recognition without needing a long vocabulary list at the start.</p><p>Use a notebook sparingly and only for words the teacher has explained. Keep each entry short: the word, a meaning agreed with the teacher and the passage where you met it. Review a small set often rather than a large set rarely.</p>
<h2>Use word roots as a memory aid</h2><p>Many Arabic words grow from a three-letter root. The root ع ل م (ʿ-l-m) is linked to knowing and teaching: <em>ʿilm</em> (knowledge) and <em>ʿalim</em> (one who knows) share it. The root س ج د (s-j-d) gives <em>sujud</em> (prostration) and <em>masjid</em> (a place of prostration). Learning a few roots makes related words easier to spot, though a teacher should confirm each meaning because patterns do not always behave the way you expect.</p>
<h2>A six-week starter routine</h2><ul><li><strong>Weeks one and two:</strong> confirm reading accuracy and collect about ten words from a passage you recite.</li><li><strong>Weeks three and four:</strong> find where those words appear elsewhere and note any shared roots.</li><li><strong>Weeks five and six:</strong> read one short passage with your word list beside a translation and bring your questions to a teacher.</li></ul>
<h2>Set expectations for pace</h2><p>Learning to understand Arabic takes sustained practice, and the pace depends on the course, your previous experience and the time you can give each week. Ask the teacher how progress will be shown, for example reading a short unfamiliar sentence with support, and avoid judging yourself against a fixed deadline.</p>
<h2>Plan for steady practice</h2><p>Language learning builds slowly. Short, regular review of vocabulary and earlier lessons usually helps more than occasional long sessions. Agree with the teacher what practice is realistic each week and when you will review the plan together. This guide does not promise fluency or a level after a fixed number of lessons. For a clear order of study, see the <a href="/blog/quranic-arabic-roadmap-first-year">Quranic Arabic roadmap</a>.</p>
<p>For interpretation or religious questions that arise while studying, ask how the college refers them to a suitably knowledgeable teacher. When you are ready, <a href="/free-trial">request a trial conversation</a> and describe what you can already read and what you hope to understand.</p>$b$)

)
update public.blog_posts p
set body_html = v.body_html,
    reading_minutes = v.reading_minutes,
    version = p.version + 1,
    updated_at = now()
from v
where p.slug = v.slug
  and p.locale = 'en'
  and p.status = 'published'
  and p.deleted_at is null
  and p.body_html is distinct from v.body_html;
